before:
value.h declares public_value, which calls a private helper in value.c. Other source files use the public operation rather than relying on the hidden helper.
```c
/* value.h */
int public_value(void);
/* value.c */
static int helper(void) { return 9; }
int public_value(void) { return helper(); }
```

Complete local C example; the app itself does not execute it:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "value.h"

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", public_value());
    return 0;
}
```

after:
Save each part in the named file. Together they form one program.
```c
// File: value.h
int public_value(void);

// File: value.c
static int helper(void) { return 9; }
int public_value(void) { return helper(); }

// File: main.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "value.h"

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", public_value());
    return 0;
}
```
